# The great sunburst on the chapel's north wall (docs/regions/krezk.md §6). sunburst: the prop's conversation and the
# Tarokka treasure spot for abbey_of_st_markovia_shrine ("Look behind the light.").

~ sunburst
Narrator: Above the altar hangs a sunburst of gilded oak as wide as a cart wheel, the brightest thing in the abbey. It hangs a little away from the wall, on two iron hooks.
if treasure_at:abbey_of_st_markovia_shrine
Narrator: When the candles below it gutter, a thin line of light shows round its edge from behind, as if there were a second sun back there, smaller and closer.
endif
* Lift it down from its hooks and look behind it. -> behind
* [Religion DC 12] (What do the rays mean?) -> rays_ok | rays_fail
* Leave it hanging. -> END

~ behind
if flag.abbot_met and not flag.abbot_fate
Narrator: The Abbot watches you climb the altar step and doesn't stop you. He only folds his hands.
Abbot: Markovia hung that. I have never moved it. Mind the hooks, children; they are older than Krezk.
endif
Narrator: It's heavy, and it groans on its hooks, and it comes down into your arms in a sift of gilt dust. Behind it, the wall has been hollowed into a niche the size of a bread oven.
if treasure_at:abbey_of_st_markovia_shrine
Narrator: Something lies in the niche, wrapped in a nun's grey veil gone soft as moss. Whoever put it there meant it to wait. It has waited.
if tarokka.tome.place == abbey_of_st_markovia_shrine
quest find_the_tome found
endif
if tarokka.symbol.place == abbey_of_st_markovia_shrine
quest find_the_holy_symbol found
endif
if tarokka.sword.place == abbey_of_st_markovia_shrine
quest find_the_sunsword found
endif
tarokka give abbey_of_st_markovia_shrine
if name:ratatoille
interject name:ratatoille: "Look behind the light." She was right. As one diviner to another, madame, I salute you.
else
interject tag:scholarly: "Look behind the light." Madam Eva knew. She knew exactly where.
endif
else
Narrator: The niche is empty except for a stub of white candle and a few grains of incense, gone to dust. Someone used to pray back here, out of sight, where only the sun could see them.
endif
Narrator: You hang the sunburst back on its hooks. It settles with a sigh.
-> END

~ rays_ok
Narrator: Twelve rays, for the twelve who marched with Markovia. One ray is shorter than the others, cut down later with a knife: for the one who turned back.
-> END

~ rays_fail
Narrator: Rays. A sun. Gilt. If it means more, it's keeping the meaning to itself.
-> END
